/*
    MS-APP-STG / Amazon RDS for SQL Server
    OPS Professional Backup, Maintenance, and Operational Monitor
    Revision 11 - one-time backup-monitor reset; sys.databases-only inventory

    What this installer creates
      - monitor schema and configuration tables in the existing OPS database
      - collection of RDS native FULL/DIFF tasks, msdb backup history,
        RDS automated transaction-log metadata, and SQL Agent outcomes
      - auto-discovery of standard Ola Hallengren maintenance jobs
      - all SQL Agent failures/cancellations from the last 12 hours
      - deadlocks from the system_health ring buffer, with XML retained in OPS
      - high-signal RDS SQL error-log entries and SQL-visible capacity anomalies
      - 90-day monitoring history
      - de-duplicated immediate failure/overdue email alerts
      - change-only highlighted HTML health report checked at 08:00 America/New_York
        (DST aware); forced reports always send
      - persistent blocking incidents for waits lasting at least 10 minutes
      - one-time deletion of OPS.monitor database/backup monitoring history
      - database inventory populated only from visible sys.databases rows
      - no creation, alteration, or ownership changes to SQL Agent jobs

    Deployment values selected by the DBA team
      Server label : MS-APP-STG
      OPS database : OPS
      Mail profile : Notifications
      Recipients   : aleksey_kokit@miopartners.com
      Thresholds  : FULL 24h, DIFF/effective data backup 6h, LOG 30m,
                    blocking 10m

    RDS notes
      - RDS SQL Agent operator notifications are not used. Email is sent by
        msdb.dbo.sp_send_dbmail.
      - RDS native FULL/DIFF tasks are asynchronous; final status is collected
        from msdb.dbo.rds_task_status.
      - RDS automated transaction-log backups are not assumed to exist in
        msdb.dbo.backupset. When available, the monitor reads
        msdb.dbo.rds_fn_list_tlog_backup_metadata.
      - SQL cannot verify an S3 lifecycle/retention rule. The report therefore
        shows backup age, task duration, S3 object ARN, and msdb expiration date
        when present. OPS monitoring history is retained for 90 days.
      - This revision never grants/denies a server permission, changes a login,
        changes a database, or creates/alters/deletes a SQL Agent job.
      - This revision intentionally does not use backup history, RDS task
        history, Ola CommandLog, deadlocks, or manual entries to discover
        database names. Only user-database rows returned by sys.databases to
        the executing login are eligible for the backup grid.

    Run this entire file in SSMS using the normal monitoring deployment login.
    OPS must already exist. The installer is idempotent and changes only
    objects in OPS.monitor. Existing SQL Agent jobs may continue to call the
    same monitor procedures; this installer does not touch those jobs.

    Design references
      - AWS RDS SQL Server Database Mail:
        https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/SQLServer.DBMail.html
      - AWS RDS transaction-log backup metadata:
        https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER.SQLServer.AddlFeat.TransactionLogAccess.Listing.html
      - AWS RDS database-name visibility:
        https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Appendix.SQLServer.CommonDBATasks.ManageView.html
      - Microsoft blocking DMV guidance:
        https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/system-dynamic-management-views/sys-dm-exec-requests-transact-sql
*/

SET NOCOUNT ON;
SET XACT_ABORT ON;
GO

USE [OPS];
GO

